snapshot.statement:
Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\mathrm{AC}_\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue, polar, Newtonian and distribution interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\Omega:=\mathbb R^3\setminus\{x:x_1=x_2=0\}$ (the complement of the $z$-axis), let $\rho(x):=\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2}$ and $\hat e_r(x):=(x_1,x_2,0)/\rho(x)$ for $x\in\Omega$ ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-metric-ball]]), let $\lambda\in\mathbb R$ be a line charge density in $\mathrm{C\,m^{-1}}$, let $r_0>0$, and define, with the constants and units of [[def-em-si-conventions]] ([[def-em-fields]], [[def-em-sources]]),
$$E(x):=\frac{\lambda\,\hat e_r(x)}{2\pi\varepsilon_0\,\rho(x)},\qquad \varphi(x):=-\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\log\frac{\rho(x)}{r_0}\qquad(x\in\Omega).$$
Then:
(a) [Gradient] $E=-\nabla\varphi$ on $\Omega$ ([[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]);
(b) [Classical equations] $\operatorname{div}E=0$ and $\operatorname{curl}E=0$ pointwise on $\Omega$; hence the fixed-time pair $(E,0)$ satisfies the reduced smooth-branch electrostatic equations with zero volume source on $\Omega$ ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (c), [[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]);
(c) [Flux per length] for every $r>0$ and $L>0$ the flux of $E$ through the lateral cylinder surface $\{x:\rho(x)=r,\ |x_3|\le L/2\}$ in the outward orientation is $\Phi_r=\lambda L/\varepsilon_0$; the parametrization $(\theta,z)\mapsto(r\operatorname{Re}e^{i\theta},r\operatorname{Im}e^{i\theta},z)$ over the rectangle $[0,2\pi]\times[-L/2,L/2]$ is an admissible regular surface patch ([[def-admissible-regular-parametrized-surface-patch]], [[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]], [[def-complex-exponential]], [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]], [[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]], [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]], [[thm-surface-area-density-is-cross-product-norm]]);
(d) [Distributional line source] extend $E$ by $0$ on the axis and let $\rho_\lambda(\psi):=\lambda\int_{\mathbb R}\psi(0,0,z)\,dz$ be the line-charge distribution on $\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-distribution]], [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]). Then, with distributional derivatives and the regular distribution of $E$ ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]], [[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]),
$$\operatorname{div}E=\frac{1}{\varepsilon_0}\rho_\lambda,\qquad\operatorname{curl}E=0\qquad\text{in }\mathcal D'(\mathbb R^3),$$
by superposition of the point-charge identities of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (d) along the axis ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]); the line density fixed by the field in this weak sense is exactly $\lambda$;
(e) [Normalization] for fixed $r_0$, $\varphi(\rho)\to-\infty$ as $\rho\to\infty$ if $\lambda>0$ and $\to+\infty$ if $\lambda<0$, while changing $r_0$ to $r_0'$ shifts $\varphi$ by $\frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\log(r_0'/r_0)$ and leaves $E$ unchanged. For $\lambda\ne0$ the infinity normalization of [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (b) is therefore unavailable; for $\lambda=0$ the field, potential and line source are zero and all cutoff energies and fluxes vanish; and $\Omega$ is not star-shaped (for every $a\in\Omega$ the segment from $a$ to $-a$ passes through the axis, which is not in $\Omega$), so [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]] does not apply to $\Omega$, although the displayed $\varphi$ still satisfies $E=-\nabla\varphi$ ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]);
(f) [Energy per length] for $0<\delta<r<R$ and $L>0$,
$$\frac{\varepsilon_0}{2}\int_{\{\delta<\rho<R,\ |x_3|<L/2\}}|E|^2\,d\lambda_3=\frac{\lambda^2L}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\log\frac{R}{\delta},$$
which for $\lambda\ne0$ diverges both as $\delta\downarrow0$ and as $R\to\infty$; there is no finite energy per unit length ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]], [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]], [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]], [[cor-volume-of-the-unit-n-ball]]);
(g) [Scope and idealization] the line charge $\rho_\lambda$ is *not* a source type of [[def-em-weak-sources]]: only point sources and stationary sheets are declared there, and no line current, moving sheet or other distribution is included. The item therefore verifies classical, flux and distributional identities for an explicitly specified distribution $\rho_\lambda$ on $\mathbb R^3$ with a noncompact singularity; it is not a framework postulate covering infinite line charges, asserts no physical realisation, equilibrium or empirical content, and is not a compact-source Coulomb corollary. Real line-like distributions have finite length and transverse size, and their far fields and energies require their own hypotheses.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\mathrm{AC}_\omega$), inherited from the cited Lebesgue and Newtonian interfaces ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $a>0$, let $\rho\in L^1(\mathbb R^3)$ be real and vanish $\lambda_3$-a.e. outside the closed ball $\overline B_a(0)$ ([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]]), let $Q$ and $p$ be the total charge and dipole moment of $\rho$ about the chart origin ([[def-em-electric-moments]] (a)), let $\|\rho\|_1:=\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\rho|\,d\lambda_3$, and let $\varphi:=\varepsilon_0^{-1}N\rho$, where $N\rho$ is the Newtonian potential of [[def-newtonian-potential]] and $\varepsilon_0$ is the constant of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Then:
(a) [Uniform monopole-dipole expansion] for every $x$ with $r:=|x|\ge2a$ the integral $\int_{\mathbb R^3}\rho(y)|x-y|^{-1}\,d\lambda_3(y)$ is absolutely convergent and equals $4\pi N\rho(x)$, and
$$\varphi(x)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r}+\frac{p\cdot x}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^3}+R(x),\qquad |R(x)|\le\frac{16\,a^2\|\rho\|_1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^3},$$
the bound being uniform over the whole exterior region $\{x:|x|\ge2a\}$;
(b) [Leading-term form] consequently, for $r\ge2a$,
$$\Bigl|\varphi(x)-\frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r}\Bigr|\le\frac{|p|}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2}+\frac{16\,a^2\|\rho\|_1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^3},$$
and if $Q=0$ and $p\ne0$, then along every direction with $p\cdot n\ne0$ the dipole term $p\cdot x/(4\pi\varepsilon_0r^3)$ is the leading term of the expansion, the remainder bound decaying one power of $r$ faster;
(c) [Physical reading] by [[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (c) the function $\varphi$ is finite $\lambda_3$-a.e., locally integrable, of class $C^\infty$ and harmonic off $\overline B_a(0)$, and satisfies $-\Delta T_\varphi=T_{\rho/\varepsilon_0}$ in $\mathcal D'(\mathbb R^3)$; the displayed expansion is therefore the exterior expansion of the potential of the compactly supported source $\rho$ in the fixed-time reading of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (e), and in the smooth branch ([[pthm-em-coulomb-solution]] (a)) the field $E:=-\nabla\varphi$ of [[def-em-fields]] realises the pure electrostatic regime of [[def-em-electrostatic-regime]] (c) with source $\rho$;
(d) [Scope and exclusions] the hypotheses are a real compactly supported $L^1$ density, the region $r\ge2a$, and the framework's constants; $\rho$ need not be continuous or bounded; the statement concerns $\varphi$ itself, and no derivative estimate for $R$, no expansion of the field $E$, and no assertion on $|x|<2a$ or on the support of $\rho$ is claimed.

Let $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^3$ be open, star-shaped ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]) and the spatial region of a pure electrostatic regime as in [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]], with fields of [[def-em-fields]], charge parameters of [[def-em-sources]] and the units of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; thus $E:\Omega\to\mathbb R^3$ is $C^1$, $B=0$ and $J=0$ ([[def-em-electrostatic-regime]]), and by [[pthm-em-electrostatic-potential]] there is $\varphi\in C^2(\Omega)$ with $E=-\nabla\varphi$. Let $q\in\mathbb R$ (coulombs) be the charge of a test particle and let $\gamma:[t_0,t_1]\to\Omega$ be a piecewise $C^1$ path ([[def-interval]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Define the **work of the electric force** along the prescribed path by the vector line integral
$$W(\gamma):=\int_\gamma qE\cdot d\mathbf r$$
of [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]]; $qE$ is the electric part of the external force adopted for a test charge in [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]](i). Then:
(a) [Value] $W(\gamma)=q\,(\varphi(\gamma(t_0))-\varphi(\gamma(t_1)))$;
(b) [Path independence] if $\gamma_1,\gamma_2$ are piecewise $C^1$ paths in $\Omega$ with the same initial and the same terminal point, then $W(\gamma_1)=W(\gamma_2)$; in particular $W(\gamma)=0$ for every closed piecewise $C^1$ path;
(c) [Potential energy] with the potential energy $U:=q\varphi$ (joules) the identity reads $W(\gamma)=-(U(\gamma(t_1))-U(\gamma(t_0)))$;
(d) [Gauge invariance of the work] if $\varphi' \in C^2(\Omega)$ is any other potential with $\nabla\varphi'=-E$, then $\varphi'=\varphi+c$ with a constant $c\in\mathbb R$ and the formula of (a) is unchanged ([[cor-potentials-differ-by-a-componentwise-constant]]).
The statement is a line-integral identity for a prescribed path in a prescribed static external field. It asserts no equation of motion, no radiation, no work done on the sources and no claim that the test particle actually follows $\gamma$; the interpretation of $W$ as work done by the adopted external coupling is the physical reading supplied by [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]], not a dynamical theorem.

Let $q\in\mathbb R$ and $a>0$, let $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be the $\delta$-orthonormal positively oriented basis of the laboratory frame ([[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-spatial-euclidean-structure-of-an-inertial-frame]]), and consider the four stationary point charges of [[def-em-electric-moments]] (c): $+q$ at $ae_1$, $+q$ at $-ae_1$, $-q$ at $ae_2$, $-q$ at $-ae_2$. Then:
(a) $Q=0$ and $p=0$; more generally every odd Cartesian moment vanishes, $M_{i_1\dots i_{2k+1}}=0$ for every $k\ge0$ and all indices, because the configuration is invariant under the central reflection $x\mapsto-x$ with the charges preserved;
(b) the degree-$2$ Cartesian moment is $M_{ij}=2qa^2\bigl(\delta_{i1}\delta_{j1}-\delta_{i2}\delta_{j2}\bigr)$, i.e. $\operatorname{diag}(2qa^2,-2qa^2,0)$ in the frame basis, and its trace vanishes;
(c) the traceless quadrupole is $Q_{ij}=6qa^2\bigl(\delta_{i1}\delta_{j1}-\delta_{i2}\delta_{j2}\bigr)=\operatorname{diag}(6qa^2,-6qa^2,0)$ and $\sum_iQ_{ii}=0$; since $Q=p=0$, it is independent of the reference point by the origin-shift theorem ([[pthm-em-moment-origin-change]] (d));
(d) [Scope] this is a finite source-moment computation for prescribed stationary point charges. No field, force, energy — in particular no claim that the self-energy of the point charges is finite —, dynamics, stability or empirical content is asserted.

Assume Countable Choice ($\mathrm{AC}_\omega$), with the exact meaning of [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $J\in C_c^\infty(\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$ with $\operatorname{div}J=0$ be a compact smooth current of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](b), extended by zero to $\mathbb R^3$, and let $\mu_0>0$, $c>0$, $\varepsilon_0=(\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ be the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Define, for $i\in\{1,2,3\}$ and $x\in\mathbb R^3$,

$$A_i(x):=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\frac{J_i(y)}{|x-y|}\,dy=\mu_0\,N(J_i)(x),\qquad A:=(A_1,A_2,A_3),$$

the componentwise Newtonian potentials of [[lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade]]. Then:

(a) $A\in C^\infty(\mathbb R^3;\mathbb R^3)$ and $-\Delta A=\mu_0J$ pointwise on $\mathbb R^3$, componentwise ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]);

(b) $\operatorname{div}A=0$ pointwise on $\mathbb R^3$: the Coulomb-gauge condition holds;

(c) the field $B:=\operatorname{curl}A$ is of class $C^\infty$ and satisfies the magnetostatic pair of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](a) pointwise on $\mathbb R^3$,

$$\operatorname{div}B=0,\qquad \operatorname{curl}B=\mu_0J ;$$

consequently $E=0$, $\rho=0$, with $B,J$ independent of time, gives an explicit full smooth-Maxwell completion on $\mathbb R^3\times I$ for every open time interval $I$ under [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]. More generally any compatible steady electrostatic pair satisfying $\operatorname{curl}E=0$ and $\operatorname{div}E=\rho/\varepsilon_0$ can be combined with $B,J$; no electrostatic existence theorem is implied;

(d) [Scope] $A$ is a vector potential for $B$ in the Coulomb gauge; the gauge condition is a construction choice and no uniqueness of $A$ or of $B$ is asserted. The construction is carried out for the compact smooth class only: nothing is claimed for the declared ideal models of [[def-em-steady-current-regime]](c), for noncompact currents or for currents of weaker regularity.

In the setting of [[pthm-em-rectangular-mode-candidates]] let a TE or TM candidate of part (a) or (b) with $\beta>0$ and $\omega>\omega_c$ be fixed (so $\beta=\sqrt{\omega^2\varepsilon\mu-k_c^2}$ is real and nonzero), let $T:=2\pi/\omega$, and let $u:=\frac12(E\cdot D+B\cdot H)$ and $S:=E\times H$ be the stored-energy density and energy-flux vector of [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]], with the Euclidean inner and cross products of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] and [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. Define the period-averaged stored energy per unit length and the period-averaged longitudinal flux across the cross-section $$U:=\frac1T\int_0^T\!\!\int_{R_{ab}}u\,dx_1dx_2\,dt,\qquad \Phi:=\frac1T\int_0^T\!\!\int_{R_{ab}}S\cdot e_z\,dx_1dx_2\,dt,$$ both independent of $z$ and of the starting time. Then:
(a) [Cross-section identities] $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_th_{mn}|^2=k_c^2\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2$ and $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_te_{mn}|^2=k_c^2\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2$, with the boundary terms of the integration by parts vanishing;
(b) [Energy and flux] for the TE candidate $$U=\frac{\mu^2\varepsilon\omega^2}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2,\qquad \Phi=\frac{\omega\mu\beta}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2,$$ and for the TM candidate $$U=\frac{\varepsilon^2\mu\omega^2}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2,\qquad \Phi=\frac{\omega\varepsilon\beta}{2k_c^2}\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2,$$ with the explicit norms of [[lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities]](c);
(c) [Energy transport and speed relations] $\Phi=U\,\beta/(\varepsilon\mu\omega)=U\,v_g$, where $v_g:=d\omega/d\beta=\beta/(\varepsilon\mu\omega)$ is the group speed of the branch $\omega(\beta)=\sqrt{(\beta^2+k_c^2)/(\varepsilon\mu)}$; moreover the phase speed $v_p:=\omega/\beta$ satisfies $v_pv_g=1/(\varepsilon\mu)$, $v_p>1/\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}$ and $0<v_g<1/\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}$ for the declared indices;
(d) [Finite propagating set] if a candidate with indices $(m,n)$ is propagating at frequency $\omega$ (that is, $\beta^2=\omega^2\varepsilon\mu-k_{mn}^2>0$), then $m<\omega a\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi$ and $n<\omega b\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi$, so the propagating indices lie in the explicitly bounded index box $\{m,n\ge0: m<\omega a\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi,\, n<\omega b\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}/\pi\}$;
(e) [Scope] This is a lossless separated-guide computation: the period average is over time at fixed position, the flux is through one cross-section, $v_p$ is not asserted to be a signal speed and no bound on information or energy speeds in general dispersive media follows; the material branch and the PEC model are those of the cited theorems.

